WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.600
MUSIC

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:27.040
The ocean, the largest habitat on Earth

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.400
and an inhospitable place for those of us who live on land.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:37.600
Yet, for a billion years,

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.280
this was the only place on the planet where life existed.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:48.880
MUSIC

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:54.160
Today, descendants of those early life forms continue to thrive.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.720
They share the sea with fish, but outnumber them by ten to one.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:08.920
They have no backbones and have evolved into countless different forms.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:17.680
Some are huge, large-brained and intelligent.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.120
Others are minuscule,

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.840
yet build the largest natural structures on the planet.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:36.280
MUSIC

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.440
They are marine invertebrates, the creatures of the deep.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:02:03.840
MUSIC

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:06.920
They have colonised every corner of the ocean

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.000
and have a mind-boggling range of solutions

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.560
to the problem of staying alive.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:21.520
MUSIC

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.240
The ocean is by no means uniform.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:39.240
Differences in depth, temperature, sunlight and currents

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.480
pose particular challenges.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.640
One and a half miles down, these hydrothermal vents

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:53.280
spew out superheated water at 450 degrees centigrade

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.560
from cracks in the Earth's crust.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:01.080
Despite the enormous pressure, the total darkness

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:03.320
and the scaldingly high temperatures,

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:09.280
the ancestors of all life may have evolved in a place just like this.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.360
Pompeii worms,

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:18.760
so named for their ability to survive volcanic heat.

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:27.920
They share the vents with crabs and two-metre-long tube worms.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:31.880
They can only survive here

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:36.640
because they're able to feed on bacteria that thrive around the vents.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:44.280
MUSIC

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.840
These colonies are extremely rare.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:54.600
Not surprisingly, most life thrives nearer the surface,

00:03:54.600 --> 00:04:05.360
where feeding is considerably easier.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:09.440
These are krill, tiny shrimp-like crustaceans.

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:15.760
Swarms can reach astounding numbers, 60,000 per cubic metre.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:20.440
During the night, they rise towards the surface to feed on plankton.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:26.600
Here, in the Sea of Cortez off Mexico,

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:29.480
the swarms attract hunters of all kinds,

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:33.160
from humpback whales to shoals of predatory fish.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.680
Yet another hunter arrives.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:37.760
It's one from the deep.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.280
A humboldt squid.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:51.080
Two metres long, they have a local reputation as man-eating.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:57.980
They're known for their

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:03.500
two-metre-long tube worms.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:08.300
Two metres long, they have a local reputation as man-eaters.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:15.380
Alone, they're formidable enough.

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:20.460
But this is a pack of hundreds.

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:26.940
They're highly intelligent hunters.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:30.780
They're reamed with 70,000 hooks and a razor-sharp beak

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:32.860
for tearing through flesh.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:42.060
Now the fish find that they are under attack

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:45.140
and so group together for safety.

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:52.140
But the humboldt squid work as a team herding the fish against the rocks.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.700
It's thought that the squid are the most dangerous

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:16.500
and it's thought that the squid flash red and white

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:18.580
not only to confuse their prey

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:44.580
but also to signal to each other when they are about to attack.

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:55.580
Humboldt squid numbers are growing rapidly.

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:58.660
But they remain mysterious.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:05.740
As fast as they arrived, they disappear back into the deep.

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:07.860
They're known for their

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:14.220
incredible

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:19.220
presence in the deep.

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:24.300
As fast as they arrived, they disappear back into the deep.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:36.740
800 miles from the South Pole,

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:44.740
the South Pole is a very dangerous place.

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:51.820
Winter temperatures are a punishing minus 40.

00:07:51.820 --> 00:08:16.820
And winter, perhaps, is the last place you might expect to find marine life.

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:20.700
Now it's the beginning of the polar spring.

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:34.700
For the first time in months, light reaches the sea beneath the ice.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:38.980
It's extremely cold and completely dark for much of the year.

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:48.460
So conditions are not unlike those of the deep ocean.

00:08:48.460 --> 00:09:10.460
Yet in McMurdo Sound, life flourishes.

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:14.100
The creatures here grow extremely slowly.

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:19.580
But that does mean they can reach a great age and great size.

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:37.180
And they occur in surprisingly large numbers.

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:41.180
Three-metre-long carnivorous nematine worms,

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:53.980
red sea stars and urchins carpet the sea floor.

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:57.700
This monster worm will eat almost anything

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:13.900
and is constantly scanning the sea floor for food.

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:19.660
Animals are swarming here in such numbers because of this.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:25.500
A dead seal pup.

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:30.420
Such a great quantity of food may only arrive once in ten years.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:40.420
But a seal's body won't be easy to eat.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:43.420
Nematines have a snout like a harpoon

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:56.340
that enables them to puncture the skin of the corpse.

00:10:56.420 --> 00:10:59.340
It's harder work for the sea stars.

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:07.140
They feed by pushing out their stomachs through their mouths.

00:11:07.220 --> 00:11:11.660
As this sea star presses its stomach against the seal's skin,

00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:16.740
it secretes digestive juices that dissolve the seal's tissue.

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:25.900
But that takes time.

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:29.140
These scavengers will feed here throughout the summer

00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:42.020
until all that remains of the seal will be a skeleton-stripped bear.

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:46.860
The shallows are only a tiny part of the marine world.

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:53.300
90% of it is open water.

00:11:53.300 --> 00:12:01.420
Its currents carry life for thousands of miles.

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:08.380
The masters of this nomadic existence are jellyfish.

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:13.020
A life spent drifting in the empty ocean could be a lonely one,

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:17.980
but not for this jellyfish.

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:21.940
Aurelia.

00:12:21.940 --> 00:12:25.260
Swarms like this are not accidental.

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:27.860
These individuals all hatched together

00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:34.540
when the temperature and currents were just right.

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:38.460
Their timing has ensured that they can make the most of feeding

00:12:38.460 --> 00:13:05.100
on a late summer plankton bloom.

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:09.060
Jellyfish have no brain and no blood,

00:13:09.140 --> 00:13:13.020
but they do have eye spots that enable them to tell the difference

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:15.100
between light and dark,

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:17.780
and they can move independently of the current

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:29.460
by a simple form of jet propulsion.

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:34.140
All jellyfish have stinging tentacles with which to catch their food

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:37.580
as they pulse their bodies, the tentacle's trapped plankton,

00:13:37.660 --> 00:14:02.700
which is then passed towards their mouth in the centre.

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:06.620
A swarm of 100,000 stinging jellyfish

00:14:06.700 --> 00:14:12.580
might seem a daunting prospect for a predator,

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:19.660
but not for this one, a huge fried egg jellyfish.

00:14:19.740 --> 00:14:25.500
It is a killer.

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:32.580
Its weapons are harpoon-like cells that cover its tentacles.

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:51.340
When they come into contact with a victim, they spear it.

00:14:51.340 --> 00:15:02.500
The fried egg then hauls in its prey.

00:15:02.580 --> 00:15:13.580
A few lucky Aurelia do manage to pull themselves free.

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:34.220
But for the majority, there's only one outcome, death.

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:44.980
Jellyfish, like a great many marine invertebrates, have soft, vulnerable bodies.

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:53.460
They are protected by stinging cells.

00:15:53.540 --> 00:16:01.180
But there are other kinds of defence.

00:16:01.180 --> 00:16:17.940
Armour plating.

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:20.780
These are spider crabs.

00:16:20.860 --> 00:16:24.740
They spend most of their lives in deep water.

00:16:24.820 --> 00:16:28.260
But once a year, off the coast of southern Australia,

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:41.140
a quarter of a million crabs set off on a long journey to the shallows.

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:44.420
They're here because they all share a problem.

00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:53.860
Each crab has been wearing the same suit of armour for a year now.

00:16:53.860 --> 00:17:01.980
And it's getting uncomfortably tight.

00:17:02.060 --> 00:17:09.220
So each crab eventually has to shed its shell and produce a bigger one.

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:17.140
Replacing an old shell is understandably a tricky process.

00:17:17.140 --> 00:17:25.900
First, the crab grows an entirely new skin within the old shell.

00:17:25.980 --> 00:17:34.740
It then flexes its body to force its shell to split along the back...

00:17:34.820 --> 00:17:46.300
before gingerly backing itself out.

00:17:46.300 --> 00:17:53.100
The spider crabs are not only here to moult, but to mate.

00:17:53.180 --> 00:18:05.420
And they grab the opportunity with considerable enthusiasm.

00:18:05.500 --> 00:18:12.860
Coming together in such numbers does, however, have a drawback.

00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:19.100
It attracts predators.

00:18:19.180 --> 00:18:35.500
A stingray.

00:18:35.580 --> 00:18:41.260
Despite there being so many potential prey, the stingray seems to ignore them.

00:18:41.260 --> 00:18:46.220
Perhaps there's safety in numbers.

00:18:46.300 --> 00:18:57.660
Although not all the crabs are prepared to rely on it.

00:18:57.740 --> 00:19:09.980
Alarm spreads amongst the crabs.

00:19:09.980 --> 00:19:12.700
But in fact, most of them are safe.

00:19:12.780 --> 00:19:14.860
This ray is being very choosy.

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:32.780
It's only interested in the softest-shelled, most recently moulted crabs.

00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:41.500
Once a target is singled out, there is no escape.

00:20:02.780 --> 00:20:05.100
When their new armour has hardened,

00:20:05.180 --> 00:20:08.620
the crabs return to the comparative safety of the deep,

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:16.300
leaving behind only their old, empty shells.

00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:20.220
Like spider crabs, the behaviour of most marine creatures

00:20:20.300 --> 00:20:24.220
